{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Kill.</b> It was necessary to say a second time ״הֲרֹגוּ״ — Rabbi Yishmael said (Sifrei) to separate, etc., for otherwise the phrase ״וְכָל אִשָּׁה״ etc. would be equally balanced whether it is drawn to what precedes or to what follows. And it is difficult, for he should have placed the word ״הַחֲיוּ לָכֶם״ in the verse ״וְכֹל הַטַּף״ before it in this manner: ״הַחֲיוּ לָכֶם״, and with this there would be no need to repeat the word ״הֲרֹגוּ״. But perhaps he was careful to say it thus, for then the intention would be that he is commanding them to let them live — and not so is the thought of HaShem; rather, he is saying it is optional, that there is no commandment to put them to death. And therefore he placed ״וְכֹל הַטַּף״ etc. first, to say that it comes only to inform what their ruling would be, not to command concerning them." "<b>Furthermore,</b> there remains for us to resolve what is still difficult: why did he say <b>הִרְגוּ</b> at the beginning of the verse, and was the ״הֲרֹגוּ״ that he said at the end not sufficient? But perhaps the intention was to juxtapose the word ״הִרְגוּ״ to the word ״וְעַתָּה״, to say that the repentance alluded to in ״וְעַתָּה״ is through the act of killing, and as long as they had not killed they had not fulfilled the repentance for the sin. Or it is possible that the intention was to juxtapose ״הִרְגוּ״ to the children and ״הֲרֹגוּ״ to the women etc., to say that there is a commandment concerning these in their own right and concerning those in their own right. Or the intention was to juxtapose the mention of killing to the word ״וְעַתָּה״, to say that they should kill them immediately and at once."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:13.691403575Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}